rfc9180_vectors.json: the test vectors of RFC 9180 (Hybrid Public Key
Encryption), Appendix A, a subset, as bench/std_hpke_exercise.sh reads
them.

Source: https://github.com/cfrg/draft-irtf-cfrg-hpke, commit
5f503c564da00b0687b3de75f1dfbdfc4079ad31 (2021-05-24, "Add test
vectors.", the last change to the file), the file test-vectors.json
(sha256 61fc662f01996cd06d713dacf5e133167bd309a1f329442d53f1e21a47b3ede6),
of which RFC 9180's Appendix A prints a part.

Changed by the iyi project, 2026-10-22: only the vectors of the suites
std/hpke has were kept - DHKEM(P-256) 0x0010 and DHKEM(X25519) 0x0020
(the file has none for DHKEM(P-384) 0x0011), HKDF-SHA256 0x0001 and
HKDF-SHA512 0x0003 (none for HKDF-SHA384 0x0002), and every AEAD and mode,
64 vectors in the file's order. Each keeps the fields its check reads
(mode, the three ids, info, ikmR, ikmE, ikmS, skRm, skEm, skSm, pkRm, pkEm,
pkSm, psk, psk_id, enc, shared_secret, key, base_nonce, and every export
as it is) and drops key_schedule_context, secret and exporter_secret, which
nothing outside the module can see. Of the 257 encryptions of a vector, the
ones at sequence 0, 1, 2, 255 and 256 are kept as they are with their
index added as "seq", and two fields are added: "count", how many the file
lists, and "ciphertexts_sha256", the SHA-256 of all their ciphertexts
concatenated in order, which the exercise checks every encryption it seals
against. The cutting checked that each vector's encryptions are its one
plaintext under aad "Count-i" at nonce base_nonce XOR i, which the
exercise assumes for the ones not kept. bench/std_hpke_exercise.sh fetches
the file whole from that commit when the network answers, checks its
digest, cuts it again by the same rule and compares the result with this
file value for value, and checks every encryption of every vector as well.

